  "summary": "The Brunswick County NC Board of Commissioners voted 5-0 to pause new ALPR purchases for 30 days and directed staff, legal, and communications to work with the Sheriff's Office on a review. The DeFlockILM article analyzes the Sept. 21, 2026 meeting, revealing in-car Axon ALPRs in every deputy vehicle, an unvoted Airship AI surveillance purchase, an alleged undisclosed vendor tie to a former sheriff, and disputed claims about audits and misuse.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "the Board voted 5\u20130 to halt procurement of any new automated license plate reader (ALPR) equipment for 30 days",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "the agreement signed November 17, 2025 puts a reader in every Sheriff's vehicle \u2014 mobile ALPR, on top of the fixed cameras across the county",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "Brunswick pays Flock for eight cameras but has fifteen in use, because Flock failed to remove seven cameras from private property as planned",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "state law does not require the audit to be performed by any independent, outside entity",
      "paragraph_idx": 35
    },
    {
      "quote": "a single Brunswick County Sheriff's Office account ran a burst of Flock searches on four vehicles. Every search gave its reason simply as \"Law enforcement.\" None carried a case number.",
      "paragraph_idx": 38
    }
  ],
